WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:06.378
AlphaFold 2 is one of the clearest
case studies for a simple idea:

2
00:00:06.445 --> 00:00:11.363
a neural architecture shaped by domain
science can matter more than big data.

3
00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:16.058
In this video we go through it step by
step, using protein folding as the lens.

4
00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:17.913
It is a companion to the slides,

5
00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.985
so feel free to pause whenever
a figure deserves a closer look.

6
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:24.763
Let's start with the problem.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.178
Proteins are the molecular
machines of life,

8
00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:30.879
and a helpful mental picture is LEGO.

9
00:00:31.129 --> 00:00:35.257
Each protein is a chain of amino acids
that folds into a specific

10
00:00:35.324 --> 00:00:40.147
three-dimensional shape, and these shapes
click together into larger assemblies.

11
00:00:40.397 --> 00:00:41.775
Shape determines function.

12
00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:45.532
So to understand what a protein
does, we need its structure.

13
00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:50.105
The first AlphaFold, from 2018,

14
00:00:50.172 --> 00:00:53.511
treated structure prediction
almost like an image problem.

15
00:00:53.761 --> 00:00:57.669
Sequence and alignment features went
into a deep convolutional network,

16
00:00:57.736 --> 00:01:03.056
which predicted a distogram: a map of
distances between every pair of residues.

17
00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:08.399
A separate optimization step then turned
those distances into a 3D structure.

18
00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:11.843
In short: a vision network
applied to distance maps.

19
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.328
How good was that? CASP is the biennial
international competition in which methods

20
00:01:18.395 --> 00:01:22.255
predict protein structures
from the amino acid sequence alone.

21
00:01:22.505 --> 00:01:23.703
For more than a decade,

22
00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:29.133
accuracy on the hardest targets hovered
around 30 to 40 on the GDT scale.

23
00:01:29.383 --> 00:01:31.899
AlphaFold 1 pushed that to almost 60.

24
00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:35.514
Two years later,
AlphaFold 2 reached about 87,

25
00:01:35.581 --> 00:01:38.445
close to the accuracy
of experimental methods.

26
00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.698
So what changed between version
one and version two?

27
00:01:42.765 --> 00:01:45.155
Almost everything about the architecture.

28
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.463
Here is the whole system on one slide.

29
00:01:48.713 --> 00:01:51.861
The input sequence is used
to search genetic databases,

30
00:01:51.928 --> 00:01:54.291
which gives a multiple sequence alignment,

31
00:01:54.358 --> 00:01:57.453
and structure databases,
which give templates.

32
00:01:57.703 --> 00:02:00.772
From these, the model
builds two representations:

33
00:02:00.838 --> 00:02:04.396
an MSA representation
and a pair representation.

34
00:02:04.646 --> 00:02:07.243
Both pass through 48 Evoformer blocks.

35
00:02:07.493 --> 00:02:11.761
Then a structure module with 8 blocks
turns them into 3D coordinates,

36
00:02:11.828 --> 00:02:14.057
together with a confidence estimate.

37
00:02:14.307 --> 00:02:17.954
Finally, the output is fed back
in and refined, three times.

38
00:02:18.021 --> 00:02:19.657
Let's unpack each part.

39
00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:23.178
First, why is this learnable at all?

40
00:02:23.245 --> 00:02:27.559
Many different amino acid sequences
fold into nearly the same shape.

41
00:02:27.809 --> 00:02:31.131
So sequence space is much
larger than structure space.

42
00:02:31.381 --> 00:02:34.665
This many-to-one mapping
actually helps generalization.

43
00:02:34.731 --> 00:02:37.800
Diverse sequences share
the same overall fold,

44
00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:41.509
even though the fine structural
details remain challenging.

45
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:46.017
This is where multiple
sequence alignments come in.

46
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:48.627
Take your protein
and stack related sequences

47
00:02:48.693 --> 00:02:52.648
from other species underneath it,
aligned position by position.

48
00:02:52.898 --> 00:02:56.583
Reading along a row gives you
information within one sequence.

49
00:02:56.833 --> 00:03:00.721
Reading down a column shows how
a single position varies across species,

50
00:03:00.788 --> 00:03:03.333
in other words, across evolution.

51
00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:08.798
AlphaFold 2 learns from this data
the way BERT learns language.

52
00:03:08.865 --> 00:03:10.623
Entries of the alignment are masked,

53
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.253
and the network is trained
to fill them back in.

54
00:03:13.503 --> 00:03:16.042
Its attention follows
the shape of the data:

55
00:03:16.108 --> 00:03:20.644
it alternates between processing
within sequences and across sequences.

56
00:03:20.894 --> 00:03:23.495
This is the recurring
theme of the whole model.

57
00:03:23.562 --> 00:03:25.785
The operations of the network are aligned

58
00:03:25.852 --> 00:03:28.505
with the biological
structure of the problem.

59
00:03:29.617 --> 00:03:34.475
You can think of AlphaFold 2 as a
translator between levels of description.

60
00:03:34.725 --> 00:03:38.273
On one side is sequence information:
a single sequence,

61
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.804
per-position statistics, pairwise
co-evolution, and the full alignment.

62
00:03:43.054 --> 00:03:47.412
On the other side is geometry:
one-dimensional features along the chain,

63
00:03:47.479 --> 00:03:49.972
a two-dimensional map
of pairwise distances,

64
00:03:50.039 --> 00:03:52.840
and finally the full 3D structure.

65
00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.940
The key inductive bias is co-evolution.

66
00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.868
In an alignment,
some columns are highly conserved,

67
00:03:59.935 --> 00:04:03.055
some are highly variable,
and some change together.

68
00:04:03.122 --> 00:04:06.977
When one position mutates,
its partner mutates to compensate.

69
00:04:07.227 --> 00:04:12.171
Positions that co-evolve like this are
usually in contact in the folded protein.

70
00:04:12.421 --> 00:04:15.379
So correlations in the alignment
let us infer contacts,

71
00:04:15.446 --> 00:04:19.967
and contacts in turn constrain
which mutations are allowed.

72
00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:24.863
In the network, this becomes two kinds
of attention over the alignment.

73
00:04:25.113 --> 00:04:27.271
Row attention works along one sequence,

74
00:04:27.338 --> 00:04:31.421
so every residue can look at every
other residue in that same sequence.

75
00:04:33.017 --> 00:04:37.005
Column attention works down
one position, across all sequences.

76
00:04:37.071 --> 00:04:39.796
It picks up the evolutionary
signal at that site.

77
00:04:41.383 --> 00:04:44.236
Alternating the two is
called axial attention.

78
00:04:44.486 --> 00:04:48.174
It is a close relative of criss-cross
attention from computer vision,

79
00:04:48.241 --> 00:04:52.800
which attends to the row and the column
of each position at the same time.

80
00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.825
Axial attention simply does all rows
first, then all columns.

81
00:04:56.891 --> 00:05:00.149
Both achieve global coverage,
but axial is simpler.

82
00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:03.358
And it cleanly separates
processing across sequences

83
00:05:03.424 --> 00:05:06.025
from processing along residue positions.

84
00:05:07.617 --> 00:05:10.517
Attention over a full
alignment is expensive.

85
00:05:10.583 --> 00:05:13.542
So during training,
AlphaFold works on crops:

86
00:05:13.608 --> 00:05:16.273
a window of the protein
instead of the whole thing.

87
00:05:16.523 --> 00:05:18.342
At inference time there is no cropping.

88
00:05:18.408 --> 00:05:20.656
The model sees the full sequence.

89
00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.078
What comes out of all this processing?

90
00:05:24.145 --> 00:05:27.044
Conceptually,
the first step is a distogram.

91
00:05:27.294 --> 00:05:30.917
It is a matrix where entry (i, j)
is the predicted distance

92
00:05:30.984 --> 00:05:35.779
between residue i and residue j,
measured between their alpha carbons.

93
00:05:36.029 --> 00:05:39.437
For a protein with L residues,
that is an L by L map,

94
00:05:39.504 --> 00:05:42.188
and it already indicates the overall fold.

95
00:05:43.783 --> 00:05:46.918
This design direction
was not an isolated idea.

96
00:05:46.985 --> 00:05:49.513
The MSA Transformer,
by Rao and colleagues,

97
00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.187
applies the same row
and column attention to alignments.

98
00:05:53.437 --> 00:05:56.225
In contact prediction,
it clearly outperformed

99
00:05:56.292 --> 00:05:59.916
protein language models
that only see single sequences.

100
00:06:01.517 --> 00:06:03.201
Now the second ingredient.

101
00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:06.526
A molecule is naturally
a graph: atoms are nodes,

102
00:06:06.592 --> 00:06:09.256
and the relationships
between them are edges.

103
00:06:09.506 --> 00:06:12.094
A standard Transformer
has tokens for the nodes,

104
00:06:12.161 --> 00:06:15.836
but no explicit place
to store information about edges.

105
00:06:16.086 --> 00:06:18.589
For proteins,
the relationships between pairs

106
00:06:18.656 --> 00:06:21.563
of residues are exactly
what we care about.

107
00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.698
Graph Transformers solve this
by adding encodings of the graph:

108
00:06:26.765 --> 00:06:30.525
spatial relations,
edge features and node centrality.

109
00:06:30.775 --> 00:06:34.567
These are injected directly
into the attention computation.

110
00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.730
AlphaFold 2 does the same with two tracks.

111
00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:43.079
The top track is the MSA representation.
Think of it as the nodes.

112
00:06:43.329 --> 00:06:45.457
The bottom track
is the pair representation,

113
00:06:45.524 --> 00:06:49.744
with one entry for every pair
of residues. These are the edges.

114
00:06:49.994 --> 00:06:54.340
In every block, the two tracks exchange
information in both directions.

115
00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:00.429
How does edge information reach
the MSA track? As a bias in attention.

116
00:07:00.495 --> 00:07:04.764
The usual score between a query
and a key gets an extra bias term,

117
00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.167
which comes from the pair representation.

118
00:07:07.417 --> 00:07:10.125
So what the model believes
about a pair of residues

119
00:07:10.192 --> 00:07:13.576
directly shapes how strongly
they attend to each other.

120
00:07:15.183 --> 00:07:19.292
And in the other direction: take
two columns of the MSA representation,

121
00:07:19.358 --> 00:07:21.428
column i and column j.

122
00:07:21.678 --> 00:07:25.405
Form their outer product,
and average it over all sequences.

123
00:07:25.655 --> 00:07:29.451
The result updates entry (i, j)
of the pair representation.

124
00:07:29.518 --> 00:07:32.947
This is co-evolution, turned
into a learned operation.

125
00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:35.765
There is a catch.

126
00:07:35.831 --> 00:07:38.510
The pair representation
describes distances,

127
00:07:38.576 --> 00:07:42.590
but so far nothing forces those distances
to be consistent with each other,

128
00:07:42.656 --> 00:07:46.151
the way real distances
in 3D space must be.

129
00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:50.373
Take three residues: i, j and k.

130
00:07:50.439 --> 00:07:53.583
If the predicted distance
between j and k is longer

131
00:07:53.649 --> 00:07:57.793
than the other two sides combined,
no such triangle can exist.

132
00:07:58.043 --> 00:08:01.392
Real distances must obey
the triangle inequality:

133
00:08:01.458 --> 00:08:04.224
each side is at most the sum
of the other two.

134
00:08:05.817 --> 00:08:10.280
So AlphaFold 2 updates each pair (i, j)
using the two other edges

135
00:08:10.347 --> 00:08:14.813
that complete a triangle with it,
summed over every third residue k.

136
00:08:15.063 --> 00:08:18.027
This does not impose
the constraint as a hard rule.

137
00:08:18.094 --> 00:08:20.422
It encourages consistency through smooth,

138
00:08:20.489 --> 00:08:23.272
learned updates
of the pair representation.

139
00:08:23.522 --> 00:08:27.550
It is expensive, because the cost grows
with the cube of the sequence length.

140
00:08:27.617 --> 00:08:29.127
But it is worth it.

141
00:08:30.717 --> 00:08:32.678
Now we need actual coordinates.

142
00:08:32.745 --> 00:08:35.208
The structure module takes
the pair representation

143
00:08:35.275 --> 00:08:38.883
together with the first row
of the MSA representation,

144
00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.485
which corresponds to our target sequence.

145
00:08:41.735 --> 00:08:42.874
And notice the counting.

146
00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.875
There are many more pairwise relationships
than coordinates to find.

147
00:08:46.942 --> 00:08:48.900
The structure is heavily constrained,

148
00:08:48.967 --> 00:08:53.400
so the model has more than enough
information to pin down the 3D shape.

149
00:08:54.983 --> 00:08:58.492
Every protein has a backbone:
a repeating chain of nitrogen,

150
00:08:58.558 --> 00:09:00.721
alpha carbon and carbon atoms.

151
00:09:00.787 --> 00:09:03.555
It is the scaffold
that the side chains hang from.

152
00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:08.285
AlphaFold treats each residue
as a rigid body.

153
00:09:08.351 --> 00:09:13.273
Its three backbone atoms form a triangle,
and that triangle defines a local frame.

154
00:09:13.523 --> 00:09:16.227
Predicting the structure now
means learning the position

155
00:09:16.293 --> 00:09:19.619
and orientation
of all these frames in space.

156
00:09:21.217 --> 00:09:23.560
Concretely,
for each residue the model learns

157
00:09:23.627 --> 00:09:27.376
a rotation matrix R
and a translation vector t.

158
00:09:27.626 --> 00:09:30.334
Together they place the local frame,
shown in yellow,

159
00:09:30.401 --> 00:09:33.316
relative to the global frame,
shown in white.

160
00:09:34.917 --> 00:09:37.625
Where do the frames start?
All in the same place.

161
00:09:37.691 --> 00:09:42.315
Every triangle sits at the origin,
oriented exactly like the global frame.

162
00:09:42.565 --> 00:09:45.358
This is the so-called
black hole initialization.

163
00:09:45.425 --> 00:09:49.621
At this point the protein is like a bag
of words, with no geometry yet.

164
00:09:51.217 --> 00:09:52.615
But there is a subtle problem.

165
00:09:52.682 --> 00:09:56.523
A protein has no meaningful
absolute coordinate system.

166
00:09:56.773 --> 00:10:00.697
Rotate or shift the whole molecule,
and it is still the same protein.

167
00:10:00.763 --> 00:10:05.387
So the model's answer must not depend
on an arbitrary choice of orientation.

168
00:10:06.983 --> 00:10:10.182
This requirement
is called SE(3) equivariance.

169
00:10:10.249 --> 00:10:12.527
If the input is rotated or translated,

170
00:10:12.594 --> 00:10:17.714
the predicted structure must rotate
or translate by exactly the same amount.

171
00:10:17.781 --> 00:10:20.089
In the diagram, both paths have to agree:

172
00:10:20.156 --> 00:10:24.473
transform first and then predict,
or predict first and then transform.

173
00:10:24.723 --> 00:10:28.754
The Evoformer works on abstract
features, so it has no such issue.

174
00:10:28.821 --> 00:10:33.188
The structure module produces real
coordinates, so it must respect this.

175
00:10:34.783 --> 00:10:37.385
The solution is Invariant Point Attention.

176
00:10:37.451 --> 00:10:40.040
The attention score keeps
its two familiar terms:

177
00:10:40.106 --> 00:10:42.736
query times key, and the pair bias.

178
00:10:42.986 --> 00:10:45.481
Then it adds a third, geometric term.

179
00:10:45.547 --> 00:10:48.486
Each residue produces points
in its own local frame,

180
00:10:48.552 --> 00:10:50.596
these are mapped into global space,

181
00:10:50.662 --> 00:10:53.499
and the distance
between them enters the score.

182
00:10:53.749 --> 00:10:57.407
Distances do not change when the whole
protein is rotated or shifted,

183
00:10:57.474 --> 00:10:59.785
so the attention is invariant.

184
00:10:59.851 --> 00:11:02.640
Queries and keys now live
in Euclidean space.

185
00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:07.568
The idea was inspired
by equivariant graph neural networks,

186
00:11:07.635 --> 00:11:12.180
which update coordinates
using relative positions between nodes.

187
00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.925
One nuance: the equivariance used
in AlphaFold 2 is approximate.

188
00:11:17.175 --> 00:11:19.009
And the lesson is pragmatic.

189
00:11:19.075 --> 00:11:21.864
You do not necessarily need
sophisticated machinery

190
00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.829
from group theory
and differential geometry.

191
00:11:26.417 --> 00:11:28.375
After Invariant Point Attention,

192
00:11:28.442 --> 00:11:31.851
each residue has an updated
single representation.

193
00:11:32.101 --> 00:11:34.729
A small network
called BackboneUpdate reads it

194
00:11:34.796 --> 00:11:37.625
and predicts a rotation and a translation.

195
00:11:37.691 --> 00:11:39.720
This is composed with the current frame,

196
00:11:39.786 --> 00:11:42.741
nudging each triangle
towards its final place.

197
00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:49.608
Putting it together: 48 Evoformer blocks,
in which row attention, column attention,

198
00:11:49.675 --> 00:11:54.093
the outer product mean and the
triangle updates repeat again and again.

199
00:11:54.343 --> 00:11:56.442
They are followed by 8
structure module blocks,

200
00:11:56.508 --> 00:11:59.265
with a loss applied after each block.

201
00:11:59.515 --> 00:12:02.373
Then the whole network runs
again on its own output.

202
00:12:02.439 --> 00:12:06.657
This recycling happens three times,
and acts as iterative refinement.

203
00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.491
Side chains come
from the same single representation.

204
00:12:11.558 --> 00:12:14.216
Because side-chain
geometry is largely rigid,

205
00:12:14.283 --> 00:12:17.717
it is enough to predict a few
torsion angles per residue.

206
00:12:17.967 --> 00:12:20.287
That gives the position of every atom.

207
00:12:20.537 --> 00:12:24.499
And the whole system, from alignment
to atoms, is trained end to end.

208
00:12:26.083 --> 00:12:27.746
How do we score a prediction?

209
00:12:27.813 --> 00:12:32.021
Local metrics such as lDDT,
the Local Distance Difference Test,

210
00:12:32.088 --> 00:12:35.273
compare distances within
each residue's neighbourhood.

211
00:12:35.523 --> 00:12:38.082
Global metrics look
at the whole structure at once.

212
00:12:38.148 --> 00:12:42.199
The distance-based RMSD
shown here is one example.

213
00:12:42.266 --> 00:12:45.974
GDT, the Global Distance Test
used at CASP, is another.

214
00:12:46.041 --> 00:12:48.212
They are related, but not the same.

215
00:12:49.817 --> 00:12:53.229
AlphaFold 2 also predicts its own lDDT.

216
00:12:53.295 --> 00:12:55.824
This predicted score, pLDDT,

217
00:12:55.890 --> 00:13:00.372
is a confidence value
between 0 and 100 for every residue.

218
00:13:00.622 --> 00:13:04.300
Blue regions are confident.
Yellow and orange regions are not,

219
00:13:04.367 --> 00:13:08.253
and they are often flexible
or unstructured in isolation.

220
00:13:08.503 --> 00:13:10.062
So you do not just get a structure.

221
00:13:10.128 --> 00:13:12.209
You get a map of where to trust it.

222
00:13:13.817 --> 00:13:16.200
Training uses a weighted sum of losses.

223
00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:19.307
The main one is FAPE,
the frame aligned point error.

224
00:13:19.374 --> 00:13:22.032
It compares atom positions
in every local frame,

225
00:13:22.099 --> 00:13:25.277
so it is invariant
to global rigid motions.

226
00:13:25.527 --> 00:13:30.044
An auxiliary loss supervises every block
during iterative refinement.

227
00:13:30.294 --> 00:13:33.872
The distogram loss supervises
the pair representation.

228
00:13:34.122 --> 00:13:37.657
The masked MSA loss
is the BERT-style fill-in objective.

229
00:13:37.907 --> 00:13:41.293
And a small confidence loss
trains the confidence estimate.

230
00:13:42.883 --> 00:13:45.907
A second, fine-tuning
phase adds two more terms.

231
00:13:46.157 --> 00:13:49.739
Not every atom has experimentally
determined coordinates.

232
00:13:49.806 --> 00:13:52.914
So AlphaFold 2 also predicts,
for each atom,

233
00:13:52.981 --> 00:13:57.463
whether it was experimentally resolved,
and this loss trains that prediction.

234
00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:59.758
Missing coordinates
are handled separately:

235
00:13:59.825 --> 00:14:04.052
the structural losses simply ignore
comparisons that would need them.

236
00:14:04.302 --> 00:14:06.890
A prediction can have
the right overall shape

237
00:14:06.957 --> 00:14:10.125
and still contain
unrealistic local geometry.

238
00:14:10.191 --> 00:14:13.930
The violation loss penalizes atoms
that come too close together,

239
00:14:13.996 --> 00:14:19.001
known as steric clashes, as well
as incorrect bond lengths and bond angles.

240
00:14:19.067 --> 00:14:22.896
During fine-tuning, it encourages
chemically plausible structures,

241
00:14:22.962 --> 00:14:26.127
even in regions without
experimental coordinates.

242
00:14:26.194 --> 00:14:29.943
These are soft penalties,
so some violations can still remain.

243
00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.746
Which of these ingredients really matter?

244
00:14:33.813 --> 00:14:37.371
The ablation study in the paper
removes them one at a time,

245
00:14:37.438 --> 00:14:41.107
and measures the change
in accuracy compared with the baseline.

246
00:14:41.174 --> 00:14:43.762
Most single removals cost a few points.

247
00:14:43.829 --> 00:14:47.803
No single trick explains the result.
It is the combination.

248
00:14:49.417 --> 00:14:52.160
The largest drop
by far comes from removing

249
00:14:52.227 --> 00:14:55.192
Invariant Point Attention
and recycling together.

250
00:14:55.442 --> 00:14:57.530
Without the geometric
term in the attention,

251
00:14:57.597 --> 00:15:01.580
and without iterative refinement,
accuracy drops sharply.

252
00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:04.178
Reasoning directly in 3D space,

253
00:15:04.245 --> 00:15:07.221
combined with refinement,
is the heart of the model.

254
00:15:08.317 --> 00:15:12.812
AlphaFold 2 showed what is possible.
OpenFold lets us study why.

255
00:15:14.417 --> 00:15:19.701
OpenFold is a faithful, trainable
reproduction of AlphaFold 2 in PyTorch.

256
00:15:19.951 --> 00:15:22.130
What sets it apart
is that the training code

257
00:15:22.196 --> 00:15:24.830
and the alignment data
are openly available,

258
00:15:24.896 --> 00:15:27.476
so the model can actually be retrained.

259
00:15:27.726 --> 00:15:32.008
That turns AlphaFold from a result
into an experimental system.

260
00:15:33.617 --> 00:15:36.105
A practical tip. To explore the code,

261
00:15:36.172 --> 00:15:40.843
append the GitHub user name and
repository name to the DeepWiki address.

262
00:15:41.093 --> 00:15:43.521
You can then ask questions
in plain language,

263
00:15:43.588 --> 00:15:47.321
for example where and how
the triangle inequality is enforced,

264
00:15:47.388 --> 00:15:49.748
and get answers linked to the source.

265
00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:52.888
Does the reproduction hold up?

266
00:15:52.955 --> 00:15:57.765
On CAMEO targets, the two models
land almost exactly on the diagonal.

267
00:15:58.015 --> 00:16:04.623
The mean scores are 0.911
for OpenFold and 0.913 for AlphaFold.

268
00:16:06.217 --> 00:16:08.685
Because we can retrain it,
we can watch it learn.

269
00:16:08.752 --> 00:16:11.245
The curve shoots up almost immediately.

270
00:16:11.495 --> 00:16:14.564
The model reaches 90 percent
of its final accuracy

271
00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:17.019
in only 3 percent of the training time.

272
00:16:17.086 --> 00:16:18.829
The rest is slow polishing.

273
00:16:20.417 --> 00:16:23.338
And now the experiment
that connects back to our title.

274
00:16:23.405 --> 00:16:26.449
What happens if you train
on deliberately reduced data?

275
00:16:26.699 --> 00:16:29.488
With only around 15 percent
of the protein chains,

276
00:16:29.554 --> 00:16:32.581
the curve ends up close
to the full-data model.

277
00:16:32.831 --> 00:16:35.970
Even with less than one percent,
about a thousand chains,

278
00:16:36.036 --> 00:16:38.543
the model still gets
most of the way there.

279
00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:41.958
Why does so little data go so far?

280
00:16:42.025 --> 00:16:45.823
Because protein fold space is rich,
but still constrained.

281
00:16:45.890 --> 00:16:48.598
Structures fall
into a limited number of classes,

282
00:16:48.665 --> 00:16:52.604
architectures and folds,
which are reused again and again.

283
00:16:52.854 --> 00:16:55.946
Nature is parsimonious
in the structures it allows.

284
00:16:56.013 --> 00:16:59.001
A model with the right
inductive biases can pick up

285
00:16:59.068 --> 00:17:02.364
these regularities
from surprisingly few examples.

286
00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.193
So, what should you take home?

287
00:17:05.443 --> 00:17:08.362
One: AlphaFold 2 turned protein folding

288
00:17:08.428 --> 00:17:11.197
into a learnable geometric
reasoning problem.

289
00:17:11.447 --> 00:17:15.316
Two: its success came
from combining co-evolution in 1D,

290
00:17:15.382 --> 00:17:20.593
pairwise reasoning in 2D, and triangle
and equivariant updates in 3D.

291
00:17:20.843 --> 00:17:24.522
Three: the architecture works
because its inductive biases

292
00:17:24.588 --> 00:17:26.896
match the biology and the geometry.

293
00:17:27.146 --> 00:17:30.294
And four: confidence prediction
and reproducibility

294
00:17:30.361 --> 00:17:32.687
made the approach scientifically useful.

295
00:17:33.783 --> 00:17:38.332
All sources are listed here, including the
lecture series this material builds on,

296
00:17:38.398 --> 00:17:42.913
the AlphaFold and OpenFold papers,
and the Nobel Prize talk.

297
00:17:42.979 --> 00:17:45.469
Pause the video if you
want to note them down.

298
00:17:46.583 --> 00:17:49.564
Thank you for watching.
Questions are very welcome.
